 L93:     * cite88†NT Forecast Areas Map L94:     * cite89†Observations L95:     * cite90†Darwin Observations L96:     * cite91†All NT Observations L97:     * cite92†Rainfall & River Conditions L98:   * cite93†Australia L99:     * cite94†Image L100:     * cite95†National Weather & Warnings L101:     * cite96†Warnings Summary L102:     * cite97†Major Cities L103:     * cite98†Radar Images L104:     * cite99†Weather Maps L105:     * cite100†Satellite Images L106:     * cite101†Rainfall & River Conditions L107: \nL108:   * cite102†Antarctica L109: \nL110:   * cite3†Bureau home L111:   * cite103†Climate L112:   * cite104†Australian climate influences L113:   * Indian Ocean\nL114: # Indian Ocean and Australian climate\nL115: \nL116:   * cite105†Indian Ocean and climate L117:   * cite106†Positive IOD impacts L118:   * cite107†Negative IOD impacts L119: \nL120: ## Sea surface temperatures\nL121: \nL122: Indian Ocean sea surface temperatures impact rainfall and temperature patterns over Australia. Warmer than average sea surface temperatures can provide more moisture for frontal systems and lows crossing Australia.\nL123: ## Indian Ocean Dipole\nL124: Sustained changes in the difference between sea surface temperatures of the tropical western and eastern Indian Ocean are known as the Indian Ocean Dipole or IOD (Indian Ocean Dipole). The IOD is one of the key drivers of Australia's climate and can have a significant impact on agriculture. This is because events generally coincide with the winter crop growing season. The IOD has three phases: neutral, positive and negative.\nL125: Events usually start around May or June, peak between August and October and then rapidly decay when the monsoon arrives in the southern hemisphere around the end of spring.\nL126:   * cite108†Neutral IOD phase L127:   * cite109†Positive IOD phase L128:   * cite110†Negative IOD phase L129: ### Neutral IOD phase\nL130: \nL131: Water from the Pacific flows between the islands of Indonesia, keeping seas to Australia's northwest warm. Air rises above this area and falls over the western half of the Indian Ocean basin, blowing westerly winds along the equator.\nL132: \nL133: Temperatures are close to normal across the tropical Indian Ocean, and hence the neutral IOD results in little change to Australia's climate.\nL134: ### Positive IOD phase\nL135: \nL136: Westerly winds weaken along the equator allowing warm water to shift towards Africa. Changes in the winds also allow cool water to rise up from the deep ocean in the east. This sets up a temperature difference across the tropical Indian Ocean with cooler than normal water in the east and warmer than normal water in the west.\nL137: Generally this means there is less moisture than normal in the atmosphere to the northwest of Australia. This changes the path of weather systems coming from Australia's west, often resulting in less rainfall and higher than normal temperatures over parts of Australia during winter and spring.\nL138: ### Negative IOD phase\nL139: \nL140: Westerly winds intensify along the equator, allowing warmer waters to concentrate near Australia. This sets up a temperature difference across the tropical Indian Ocean, with warmer than normal water in the east and cooler than normal water in the west.\nL141: \nL142: A negative IOD typically results in above-average winter–spring rainfall over parts of southern Australia as the warmer waters off northwest Australia provide more available moisture to weather systems crossing the country.\nL143: ## Monitoring the IOD\nL144: \nL145: cite111†Image: Map of Niño and IOD (DMI) regions The IOD index (or Dipole Mode Index, DMI) is used to identify IOD phases, by taking the difference in SST temperature anomalies between the west and east regions in the Indian Ocean.\nL146: IOD regions:\nL147: \nL148:   * IOD west: 50°E to 70°E and 10°S to 10°N\nL149:   * IOD east: 90°E to 110°E and 10